.TH PKGS 1 "October 2026" "pkgs 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pkgs \- fuzzy package management for Termux and Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pkgs
[COMMAND] [OPTIONS] [NAMES...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Bare invocation displays help. Install and uninstall open an fzf multi-select
picker when package names are omitted. Exact names bypass the picker.
Transactions show manager, scope and commands. Install, upgrade and refresh authorize unattended
execution with native confirmation flags; remove/restore retain confirmation.
Accepting an install-picker selection authorizes installation; cancelling installs nothing.
Dry-run never runs mutation commands or recipe hooks.
Human upgrades show one result per manager, transient progress, short diagnostics
and skip counts. Routine manager output and full command arrays are hidden.
Use --raw for full native output or --dry-run for complete plans and skip reasons.
Ordinary redirected output retains its full format. Child terminal settings are
restored on completion or interruption.
Pip progress shows the package, download bytes with pip 25.1 or newer, build
stages and elapsed silence after 15 seconds without output. Silence does not
prove a hang or cause termination. Explicit native progress overrides are kept.
Selected global npm upgrades use targeted npm install commands to avoid checking
unrelated platform-specific aliases. Project upgrades retain npm update;
ordinary global commands do not add --force.
Compact npm output shows native metadata, cache/fetch and install-script events,
with elapsed silence after 15 seconds. The child receives an info logging default;
explicit logging flags/environment win. Progress hides URLs and script commands.
Native script approval remains unchanged. Raw and redirected environments are kept.
Help, transaction previews, list, managers and doctor use shared Dots presentation in terminals and
menu readers: semantic headings/statuses, readable fields and resource paths.
Doctor expands nested FZF/completion details into sections. Plain popup reports
retain their layout. Ordinary redirected formats and JSON remain unchanged.
Formatted APT output suppresses only the unstable-CLI advisory. Raw forwarding
retains all bytes. Slow exports report manager progress on terminal stderr;
stdout remains JSON. Python root ownership uses batched APT queries.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B in, un, up, xup, upls
Aliases for install, uninstall, upgrade, export --update and upgrade --list.
.TP
.B install [NAMES...]
Install exact names or open the package picker.
.TP
.B uninstall [NAMES...]
Remove installed packages only. Alias: remove.
.TP
.B search [QUERY]
Read-only picker; Enter shows information.
.TP
.B list [\-\-installed] [\-\-json]
Show package inventory as readable text or JSON.
.TP
.B info NAMES...
Colorized package information, wrapped to fit the terminal preview.
.TP
.B upgrade [NAMES...]
Without names or an explicit manager, upgrade supported user-wide scopes:
one system database, global npm, uv tools, pip-owned base distributions,
user RubyGems and Go tools. No extra --yes is required without a terminal.
Explicit manager/names retain selected scope. Independent failures continue
and return nonzero; completed package changes remain.
Go upgrades query latest module versions and use go install PACKAGE@latest for
newer eligible tools only. Current/newer builds are left alone. Dry-run performs
bounded read-only version checks without installation. Custom flags remain literal;
explicit replacement recipes own their update semantics. The helper is not selected
automatically. Compact progress names each binary before native output begins and
shows quiet time after 15 seconds; raw output retains full commands and messages.
.TP
.B upgrade ls | upgrade \-\-list
List available updates by manager with current and candidate versions. --json
prints schema-1 per-manager status, packages and skips. Never install or execute
recipes. System queries use cached indexes; language queries may contact registries.
Query failures remain errors. Use upgrade -- ls to target a package named ls.
.TP
.B cleanup [FILE] [\-\-dry-run | \-\-json]
Review disabled source/editable pip entries from the current profile manifest
or FILE. Require current pip ownership and negative system ownership evidence.
Preview, select through FZF and confirm before native removal. --yes cannot
bypass review. Recheck file/config/profile guards and installed provenance.
Retain original source trees and manifest records; verify removed packages
are absent. Dry-run and JSON only inspect. Package removal has no Dots undo.
.TP
.B format [\-\-manager NAME]
Format stdin with semantic colors and full unknown messages. --raw preserves
bytes. Use shell pipeline status support to retain the producer's exit status.
.TP
.B export [\-\-output FILE] [\-\-update] [\-\-include-dependencies] [\-\-pin-versions]
Export editable schema-1 JSON. Prefer explicit roots; label inferred inventories.
Observed versions are separate from optional supported pins. Snapshot replacement
without --update uses guarded backups and confirmation. Partial export does not replace a file.
--update defaults to packages.json beside the current profile.json. Refresh
observed versions and add installed entries while preserving pins, disabled
entries and absent desired packages. The explicit update authorizes the guarded
write without --yes or a controlling terminal, retaining backups and stale-input
checks. --dry-run prints the proposed JSON without writes. Package
menu prompts use this profile path and Escape returns without a write.
Dots publication can include this update explicitly with git publish --update-packages.
.TP
.B manifest FILE [\-\-json]
Inspect/validate a manifest without querying package managers.
.TP
.B restore FILE [\-\-dry-run] [\-\-yes] [\-\-json]
Install missing packages, retain installed unpinned versions and enforce supported
pins. Never remove unlisted packages. System groups precede languages.
.TP
.B refresh
Refresh repository metadata for APT, DNF and XBPS without an extra confirmation
flag. Use upgrade with pacman.
.TP
.B managers, doctor
Show detected managers or diagnose the environment.
.TP
.B completion SHELL [install|update|status|uninstall]
Print or manage completions for bash, zsh, or fish. --install aliases install.
.TP
.B cache clear
Remove pkgs catalog caches; does not touch package-manager caches.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-H, \-\-help
Show help.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Show version.
.TP
.B \-\-manager NAME
Use apt, pacman, paru, yay, dnf, xbps, npm, uv-tool, pip, gem, go, or auto.
.TP
.B \-\-query TEXT
Search/filter the picker. Do not combine with exact names.
.TP
.B \-\-global
Select npm global scope. Default npm scope is the current project.
.TP
.B \-\-color MODE
Choose auto, always, or never. Auto respects NO_COLOR.
For list/managers/doctor reports, explicit --color overrides DOTS_COLOR, then
configured color, then auto. Always explicitly overrides NO_COLOR.
DOTS_ICONS independently controls report resource icons.
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-preview LAYOUT
fzf preview-window layout. Default: bottom:55%:wrap.
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Display transaction without changing packages.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-yes
Approve removal, restore or file replacement. Cleanup still requires interactive
selection and confirmation and refuses this flag.
.TP
.B \-\-directory DIR
Completion destination directory. Bash and Zsh installation is file-only.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Print read-only preparation phase timings to stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Forward subprocess or formatter input bytes without styling.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore-skip
Explicitly override direct-target exclusions in native policy.
.SH PACKAGE POLICIES
Native pkgs/config.json owns upgrade.managers (auto or ordered manager list),
output (auto/styled/raw), and policies. Per-manager policies support skip,
install/upgrade flags and before/replace/after argv sequences, plus package
rules. Commands are literal arrays without implicit shell evaluation.
Whole-token {packages} expands target names; {package} is per-package only.
Recipes are trusted executable configuration and never run during dry-run.
Dots Config exposes apps.pkgs with guarded native edits and retained undo.
Skips are not dependency holds. See docs/packages.md in the Dots checkout.
.SH PORTABILITY
Native Windows supports help/version, format and manifest, with Config editing
through Dots. Package operations return status 3 without launching WSL.
Optional Dots setup --packages FILE restores declared packages before files;
failed restoration prevents configuration writes. File rollback never removes
successful packages. Native Windows refuses that optional package stage.
.SH COMPLETION
Install/update writes _pkgs for Zsh or pkgs for Bash without adding startup
blocks, fpath changes, source statements, compinit, or compdef calls.
Fish retains its completion file and managed conf.d setup.
.br
Zsh uses an explicit directory, a recorded destination, or caller-supplied
FPATH. From Zsh: FPATH="$FPATH" pkgs completion zsh install.
The child process cannot inspect the parent shell's live fpath or completion state.
.br
Bash requires a recognized bash-completion loader for automatic resolution.
It respects BASH_COMPLETION_USER_DIR, otherwise XDG_DATA_HOME's user completion
directory. Installing the file does not load bash-completion in the current shell.
An explicit directory permits file-only installation with activation unverified.
.br
The application installer accepts repeatable --completion-dir SHELL=DIR.
Unknown destinations fail preflight without changing startup files. Status
reports the file, content/version, resolution, loader and activation separately.
Reload instructions are printed, never written into startup files.
.br
Exact legacy pkgs blocks are removed after backing up the startup target;
symlinks and unrelated content are preserved. Edited/malformed blocks and
foreign or edited completions are left untouched and reported.
.SH PICKER
Tab/Shift-Tab select packages. Enter accepts. Ctrl-X toggles the preview.
Alt-I hides/shows installed packages. Alt-O opens the highlighted homepage.
Esc/Ctrl-C cancel. [i] marks installed packages; [e] marks an unowned related
executable (same-name matches are candidates, not proof). Rows and list use
cached external evidence. A blank marker does not prove no external command
exists. Highlighting a package fetches current evidence in the preview without
blocking initial picker startup. Source-built ~/.fzf/bin/fzf remains supported.
.br
Read-only preparation queries have a 45-second budget; preview enrichment has
five seconds and individual ownership queries at most three seconds. Slow work
prints delayed stderr progress. Esc/Ctrl-C cancels queries and preview children.
Interactive package transactions retain native prompts and have no such timeout.
.SH FILES
~/.config/pkgs/config.json (XDG_CONFIG_HOME respected).
.br
~/.cache/pkgs/ (XDG_CACHE_HOME respected).
.br
~/.local/state/pkgs/completions.json (XDG_STATE_HOME respected); records managed
completion destinations for subsequent update/uninstall.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
FZF_DEFAULT_OPTS and FZF_DEFAULT_OPTS_FILE retain compatible fzf preferences.
NO_COLOR disables automatic ANSI colors. PKGS_FUZZY_TAB=0 requests native
completion without the bundled fuzzy Tab integration. Native candidate generation
uses local data with a 1.5-second query budget. PKGS_DEBUG=1 enables helper
stderr diagnostics. FPATH may be supplied by the invoking Zsh for one command.
Picker helpers use a controlled noninteractive Bash child without startup hooks.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Registry catalogs are not exhaustive. pip/uv queries use exact PyPI names.
Completion never searches remote registries. Native Windows is not supported.
.SH SEE ALSO
fzf(1), apt(8), pacman(8), dnf(8), xbps-query(1)

.SH NATIVE WINDOWS IN DOTS
The bundled Dots revision supports the WinGet community source through Microsoft.WinGet.Client.
Use --manager winget with --scope user (default) or explicit --scope machine.
Machine mutations require an already elevated shell. Package IDs and versions are exact.
Ambiguous or uncorrelated installed entries remain disabled. No implicit source agreement,
provider bootstrap, trust change, reboot or WSL invocation occurs.
Native pickers use shared Gum or numbered selection; PowerShell completion is provided by Dots.
